# 1. ABSOLUTE RULES — MUST FOLLOW

These rules are mandatory throughout the entire task.

### Rule 1: Preserve the Existing Design

DO NOT change:

- Existing UI/UX design.
- Layout, structure, spacing, or alignment.
- Colors, fonts, typography, or themes.
- Buttons, cards, tables, forms, and navigation styles.
- Existing animations and transitions.
- Header, footer, sidebar, navbar, or dashboard design.
- Existing responsive layouts.
- Existing images, icons, and visual assets.

Do not redesign or modernize the interface.

Do not replace existing components just because you prefer another design.

Only make the smallest necessary UI changes when an existing bug makes the application unusable. Even then, preserve the original visual appearance.

### Rule 2: Do Not Rebuild the Project

- Do not create a new project.
- Do not replace the existing framework.
- Do not rewrite the complete codebase.
- Do not replace working components unnecessarily.
- Do not remove existing features.
- Do not change established application architecture without a proven technical necessity.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.
- Do not upgrade packages without checking compatibility and necessity.

Work with the existing technology stack, architecture, coding conventions, and project structure.

### Rule 3: Protect Existing Data and Functionality

- Never delete existing user data.
- Never overwrite production data with test data.
- Never execute destructive database commands on production.
- Never remove existing database tables or columns without a verified migration plan.
- Never expose API keys, passwords, secrets, or environment variables.
- Never disable security features just to make tests pass.
- Never fake successful operations.
- Never hide or ignore errors.

Before editing files, inspect the Git status and existing changes. Preserve all user-created or uncommitted work.

### Rule 4: Fix Problems, Not Just Symptoms

Identify the actual root cause of each problem.

Implement the smallest safe and maintainable fix.

After fixing an issue, test the affected functionality and check for regressions elsewhere.

### Rule 5: Never Claim an Untested Feature Works

Only mark a feature as working after successfully testing it.

If a feature cannot be tested because of missing credentials, unavailable services, or environment limitations, clearly report it as BLOCKED or NOT VERIFIED.

Do not fabricate test results.

